Implement Gitea Issue #866: `test(worker): increase high-impact worker coverage to 80%+`.

Repository: Sport-Analytics-Tool

IMPORTANT:
- The authoritative repository and tracker are on Gitea.
- The GitHub repository is only a mirror.
- Do NOT push to, open PRs against, or commit directly to the GitHub mirror.
- Multiple developers are working on this repository. Preserve unrelated work and do not overwrite changes from other contributors.

## Before changing anything

1. Read and follow all applicable repository/project guidance, including:
   - `AGENTS.md`
   - `AGENTS.override.md` if present
   - repository Git methodology
   - project methodology
   - testing/coverage documentation
   - AI policy
   - AI evidence/register guidance
   - worker-specific README/guidance
   - relevant files under `docs/`

2. Inspect Gitea Issue #866 and treat its acceptance criteria as authoritative.

3. Check:
   - current branch;
   - working tree;
   - remotes;
   - latest authoritative `origin/main`.

4. Fetch the latest Gitea `origin/main`.

5. Create/work on:

   `test/866-worker-high-impact-coverage`

6. Base the branch on the latest `origin/main`.

Do not assume historical coverage figures are still exact until you measure the current branch.

---

# Objective

Increase meaningful direct automated coverage of the worker workspace.

Historical baseline:

- Worker lines: 61.46%
- Worker statements: 58.81%
- Worker functions: 53.81%
- Worker branches: 54.84%

Historical repository-wide baseline after the previous coverage work:

- Lines: 77.63% (9,066 / 11,678)
- Statements: 76.33% (9,436 / 12,362)
- Functions: 82.18% (2,408 / 2,930)
- Branches: 70.47% (6,515 / 9,245)

Primary target:

**Worker line coverage >= 80%**

Preferred worker target:

**82–85% lines**, if achievable through useful behavioural tests.

Repository-wide target:

**>80% line coverage**

At the historical denominator of 11,678 lines, the repository needed at least 277 additional covered lines to cross 80%.

Do NOT rely on that calculation after fetching current main. Measure the actual current baseline first.

The aim is to get the repository comfortably above 80%, not barely over the threshold.

---

# Critical constraints

Do NOT improve coverage by:

- changing `coverage.include`;
- narrowing the coverage denominator;
- excluding worker files;
- adding `istanbul ignore`;
- adding `c8 ignore`;
- adding equivalent coverage suppression;
- deleting production code solely to increase coverage;
- exporting private helpers purely for tests;
- restructuring production files purely for coverage;
- weakening assertions;
- increasing timeouts merely to conceal flaky or slow tests;
- adding tests that only assert "does not throw";
- adding tests whose only meaningful effect is executing lines.

Production behaviour should remain unchanged unless a genuine defect is discovered.

If an unrelated defect is discovered:
- identify it explicitly;
- do not silently change unrelated behaviour;
- recommend a separate issue unless a minimal fix is truly required for #866.

---

# Stage 1 — establish the live baseline

Before writing tests, run:

`npm run test:coverage --workspace=@sport-analytics/worker`

Record:

- lines;
- statements;
- functions;
- branches.

Inspect the per-file worker report.

Also run:

`npm run test:coverage`

Record the current repository-wide aggregate counters.

Do not begin by blindly following historical percentages if current main has changed.

---

# Historical high-impact worker gaps

Use these as starting points only.

The previous report showed:

- `batch-validation-job.ts`: ~31.49% lines
- `runtime-dependencies.ts`: ~28.57% lines
- database delivery receiver: ~13.51% lines
- `outbox-relay.ts`: ~61.70% lines
- `batch-publication-job.ts`: ~67.36% lines

The worker overall was ~61.46% lines.

The largest opportunity is expected to be `batch-validation-job.ts`.

---

# Stage 2 — Priority 0: batch-validation-job.ts

Target:

`apps/worker/src/batch-validation-job.ts`

Inspect the entire real public/exported contract before deciding what tests to write.

Do not invent behaviour based on this prompt.

Add meaningful direct behavioural tests for important currently uncovered paths, including where actually implemented:

- validation job startup;
- batch/package loading;
- chunk loading;
- package validation;
- item validation;
- validation result aggregation;
- accepted/rejected item handling;
- malformed inputs;
- unsupported input state;
- reference scanning;
- canonical reference lookup;
- participant reference mapping;
- fixture reference mapping;
- innings reference handling;
- unresolved reference creation;
- ambiguous reference handling;
- reviewer-actionable references;
- persisted reference-resolution decisions;
- resolution overrides;
- fixture onboarding tasks;
- participant onboarding/reference tasks;
- competition-scope validation;
- correction target resolution;
- correction snapshots;
- correction conflicts;
- replacement/revision relationships where relevant;
- partial failures;
- retryable failures;
- permanent failures;
- state transitions;
- batch validation completion;
- publication eligibility;
- failure-state persistence;
- safe error details;
- replay/idempotency;
- bounded chunk processing;
- deterministic processing/order;
- transaction/query sequencing observable from the worker boundary.

Cover real worker orchestration behaviour, not shared-package internals that are already well tested elsewhere.

The `batch-processing` workspace already has strong coverage. Avoid duplicating its unit tests unnecessarily.

Worker tests should primarily prove:

- orchestration;
- dependency interactions;
- state transitions;
- persistence calls;
- retry behaviour;
- failure classification;
- idempotency;
- bounds.

---

# Stage 3 — Priority 1: runtime-dependencies.ts

Target:

`apps/worker/src/runtime-dependencies.ts`

Historical coverage: ~28.57%.

Cover meaningful runtime composition behaviour including where actually supported:

- database dependency creation;
- object store creation;
- batch-processing dependency wiring;
- environment-driven composition;
- required provider selection;
- optional integrations;
- invalid/missing configuration;
- dependency reuse;
- dependency cleanup;
- creation failures;
- shutdown-related behaviour;
- provider-specific configuration.

Prefer behaviour-focused assertions over implementation details.

Do not mock so deeply that the test no longer proves anything useful.

---

# Stage 4 — Priority 1: database delivery receiver

Identify the exact current worker file corresponding to the database delivery receiver.

Historical line coverage: ~13.51%.

Cover meaningful behaviour such as:

- fetching available work;
- successful receipt;
- successful processing;
- acknowledgement;
- failed processing;
- retry/requeue decisions;
- empty queue/work set;
- malformed payload;
- missing job;
- duplicate/idempotent delivery;
- bounded reads;
- ordering;
- database errors;
- transaction behaviour where exposed;
- cancellation/shutdown where supported.

Use the actual implementation as the source of truth.

---

# Stage 5 — Priority 1: outbox-relay.ts

Target:

`apps/worker/src/outbox-relay.ts`

Historical line coverage: ~61.70%.

Cover meaningful uncovered paths including where supported:

- empty outbox;
- pending outbox records;
- successful relay;
- failed relay;
- retry;
- acknowledgement sequencing;
- update sequencing;
- bounded batches;
- partial failure;
- duplicate delivery protection;
- stop/shutdown behaviour;
- database errors;
- downstream delivery errors;
- continuation after recoverable errors.

Verify semantic behaviour rather than exact implementation strings.

---

# Stage 6 — Priority 1: batch-publication-job.ts

Target:

`apps/worker/src/batch-publication-job.ts`

Historical line coverage: ~67.36%.

Cover important worker-level publication orchestration including where supported:

- normal publication;
- chunked publication;
- publication checkpointing;
- resuming from checkpoints;
- already-completed work;
- leases/claims;
- retry behaviour;
- idempotent replay;
- partial publication;
- publication failure;
- correction publication;
- statistics refresh interaction;
- final state persistence;
- malformed/missing state;
- downstream failure;
- cleanup/finalisation.

Do not duplicate `packages/batch-processing` tests unnecessarily.

---

# Stage 7 — secondary targets

Only if needed after the high-impact modules:

- `probe-job.ts`
- `config.ts`
- `health.ts`
- `delivery-pump.ts`
- `azure-blob-object-store.ts`
- worker runtime/entrypoint paths that are meaningfully testable
- any other current high-uncovered worker file revealed by the live coverage report

Do not spend time raising files from 90% to 100% while large business-critical files remain near 30%.

---

# Test design

Prefer focused behavioural unit tests around exported worker APIs/functions.

Use deterministic fake/mock boundaries for:

- database access;
- object storage;
- queue/job input;
- batch-processing functions;
- downstream publication;
- time only where necessary;
- external providers.

Assertions should prove meaningful effects such as:

- state transitions;
- exact domain outcomes;
- calls to dependencies;
- dependency call ordering;
- persisted values;
- retry decisions;
- idempotency;
- bounded/chunked processing;
- failure classification;
- absence of unsafe follow-up calls after failure.

Where SQL/database behaviour is mocked, assert:

- meaningful parameters;
- relevant query semantics;
- call order;
- result mapping.

Avoid large byte-for-byte SQL snapshots unless exact SQL text is part of the contract.

---

# Organise the tests sensibly

Do not build one giant test file.

Prefer focused files following existing project conventions.

Possible examples, only if they fit the current repository conventions:

- `apps/worker/tests/batch-validation-job.test.ts`
- `apps/worker/tests/runtime-dependencies.test.ts`
- `apps/worker/tests/database-delivery-receiver.test.ts`
- `apps/worker/tests/outbox-relay.test.ts`
- `apps/worker/tests/batch-publication-job.test.ts`

Reuse existing test files when that produces a cleaner structure.

Do not introduce an elaborate new worker testing framework just for #866.

---

# Coverage-guided workflow

Work iteratively:

1. Measure the current worker baseline.
2. Inspect largest uncovered areas.
3. Understand the real behaviour.
4. Add focused tests.
5. Run the focused test file.
6. Rerun worker coverage.
7. Decide the next highest-value target.
8. Repeat.

After each significant group:

`npm run test:coverage --workspace=@sport-analytics/worker`

Suggested order:

1. `batch-validation-job.ts`
2. `runtime-dependencies.ts`
3. database delivery receiver
4. `outbox-relay.ts`
5. `batch-publication-job.ts`
6. secondary targets only if needed

Do not wait until the end to discover that tests had little coverage impact.

---

# Target strategy

Do not stop the moment the worker reaches 80.00%.

Aim for approximately:

**82–85% worker line coverage**

if additional worthwhile tests exist.

The main repository target should end comfortably above 80%, ideally with at least a modest buffer.

Do not add low-value tests solely to chase an arbitrary 85% if meaningful coverage has already been achieved.

---

# Preserve existing strong workspaces

Historical line coverage:

- Frontend: 81.61%
- Backend: 76.21%
- Worker: 61.46%
- Contracts: 96.34%
- Batch processing: 89.29%
- Repository total: 77.63%

Do not make changes that reduce coverage in frontend, backend, contracts, or batch-processing merely to improve worker coverage.

---

# Verification

Once the worker reaches the target, run:

`npm run test:coverage --workspace=@sport-analytics/worker`

Then:

`npm run test:coverage`

Then:

`npm run check`

Then:

`npm run hygiene`

Then:

`git diff --check`

Also run any additional focused checks required by repository guidance.

Do not report PASS unless the command actually completed successfully.

---

# Timeout handling

If a worker test times out:

1. investigate the actual cause;
2. run the test individually;
3. run it under coverage instrumentation where practical;
4. determine whether the problem is:
   - leaked timers;
   - leaked handles;
   - instrumentation overhead;
   - database/mock behaviour;
   - accidental infinite retry;
   - test concurrency;
   - environment contention;
   - genuine slow behaviour.

Do not globally raise test timeouts merely to hide a problem.

If an existing unrelated infrastructure limitation remains, document it accurately.

---

# Repository-wide coverage

Use:

`npm run test:coverage`

for the final repository figure.

Do NOT calculate final repository coverage by averaging workspace percentages.

Record exact counters:

- lines covered / total;
- statements covered / total;
- functions covered / total;
- branches covered / total.

Compare the final values with the measured baseline.

Primary repository acceptance target:

**repository-wide lines >80%**

Preferred result:

comfortably above 80% rather than exactly at the threshold.

---

# Evidence

Create:

`evidence/validation/issue-866-worker-high-impact-coverage.md`

Include:

## Baseline

- worker lines;
- worker statements;
- worker functions;
- worker branches;
- repository-wide lines;
- repository-wide statements;
- repository-wide functions;
- repository-wide branches.

## Final

Record the same metrics after implementation.

## Per-file impact

Include before/after coverage for major targets such as:

- `batch-validation-job.ts`
- `runtime-dependencies.ts`
- database delivery receiver
- `outbox-relay.ts`
- `batch-publication-job.ts`

## Tests added

Record:

- test files added/changed;
- production files exercised;
- behaviours protected;
- failure paths protected;
- retry/idempotency paths protected;
- major remaining worker coverage gaps.

## Verification

Record the exact result of:

- worker coverage;
- repository coverage;
- `npm run check`;
- `npm run hygiene`;
- formatting/diff checks;
- focused commands.

## Integrity

Explicitly confirm whether any of the following were introduced:

- coverage exclusions;
- coverage-ignore comments;
- denominator/configuration changes;
- production restructuring solely for coverage;
- test-only exports;
- weakened assertions;
- timeout increases.

Expected result: none.

Do not fabricate percentages, commands, timestamps, commit hashes, issue links, PR links, or outputs.

---

# AI policy

Follow the COMS3011A/project AI policy exactly.

Inspect the existing schema before editing:

`evidence/ai/registers/nadav-sundy.csv`

If Nadav is implementing #866, add/update the appropriate AI usage entry.

Record truthfully:

- tool;
- actual model;
- purpose;
- Issue #866;
- implementation/test work;
- review/adaptation;
- evidence/transcript reference where actually available.

Do not invent transcript filenames.

If AI generated code/tests, the commit must contain the required:

`Assisted-by: ...`

trailer using the ACTUAL model/tool used.

For example, only if accurate:

`Assisted-by: Codex[GPT-5]`

Do not blindly use that exact model string if the actual Codex model differs.

---

# Acceptance Criteria

Do not declare #866 complete unless all applicable criteria are satisfied:

- [ ] Current baseline measured from latest `origin/main`.
- [ ] Worker line coverage >=80%.
- [ ] Preferred 82–85% worker target pursued where meaningful.
- [ ] Repository-wide line coverage >80%.
- [ ] Repository-wide result has a reasonable buffer where practical.
- [ ] `batch-validation-job.ts` receives substantial meaningful direct coverage.
- [ ] At least two additional important under-covered worker modules receive meaningful direct coverage.
- [ ] Important success paths tested.
- [ ] Important failure paths tested.
- [ ] Retry behaviour tested where applicable.
- [ ] Idempotency/replay behaviour tested where applicable.
- [ ] State transitions tested where applicable.
- [ ] Chunk/boundary behaviour tested where applicable.
- [ ] Dependency sequencing tested where applicable.
- [ ] Tests contain meaningful behavioural assertions.
- [ ] No new coverage exclusions.
- [ ] No coverage-ignore directives.
- [ ] No denominator manipulation.
- [ ] No production restructuring solely for coverage.
- [ ] No timeout inflation to hide failures.
- [ ] Existing worker tests continue to pass.
- [ ] Worker coverage command passes.
- [ ] Repository-wide coverage command passes.
- [ ] `npm run check` passes.
- [ ] `npm run hygiene` passes.
- [ ] `git diff --check` passes.
- [ ] Other strong workspace coverage does not regress because of this work.
- [ ] Evidence contains real before/after results.

---

# Before finishing

Review the complete diff.

Check for:

- accidental production changes;
- debug output;
- `.only`;
- `.skip`;
- skipped test suites;
- coverage-ignore directives;
- test-only exports;
- overly broad mocks;
- weak assertions;
- copied/duplicated fixtures;
- generated coverage artifacts accidentally staged;
- secrets;
- credentials;
- unrelated files.

Run:

`git status`

`git diff --check`

`git diff --stat`

Inspect the actual diff before declaring the implementation ready.

---

# Git rules

Do NOT:

- commit directly to `main`;
- push directly to `main`;
- merge;
- push;
- create a PR;
- close Issue #866;
- modify the GitHub mirror;

unless I explicitly ask you to do so.

Leave the implementation ready for human review.

You may create a local commit only if I explicitly authorize it.

---

# Final response

When implementation is complete, give me:

1. exact files changed;
2. exact tests added/changed;
3. behaviours now covered;
4. baseline worker:
   - lines;
   - statements;
   - functions;
   - branches;
5. final worker:
   - lines;
   - statements;
   - functions;
   - branches;
6. baseline repository-wide coverage;
7. final repository-wide coverage;
8. exact covered-line gain;
9. major per-file coverage improvements;
10. verification commands and exact outcomes;
11. remaining important uncovered worker behaviour;
12. any defects discovered;
13. confirmation that no coverage manipulation was used;
14. whether every locally applicable acceptance criterion is satisfied;
15. suggested PR title;
16. suggested commit message.

Suggested PR title:

`#866 Increase high-impact worker coverage above 80%`

Suggested commit message:

`test(worker): increase high-impact coverage above 80%

Refs #866
Assisted-by: Codex[ACTUAL MODEL]`

Do not claim completion unless:

- worker lines are >=80%;
- repository-wide lines are >80%;
- the actual coverage commands pass;
- the quality gates pass.

